WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.540
A recall lets voters remove an elected official before

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.500
the term ends.

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.280
Proposition five changes what happens next.

4
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.480
It does not eliminate the power to recall state

5
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.020
officials.

6
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:18.220
Today, a state recall ballot normally asks two questions:

7
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.440
whether to remove the official, and who should replace

8
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:21.840
them.

9
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.940
If removal wins, the replacement candidate with the most

10
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.340
votes takes office.

11
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.300
Proposition five removes the replacement question.

12
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.480
The office would be filled through succession, appointment, or

13
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.560
a separate election, depending on the office and when

14
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:38.600
the recall happens.

15
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.960
Imagine opening a ballot during a recall election.

16
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.520
In a hypothetical crowded replacement contest, one candidate could

17
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.680
lead with thirty votes out of a hundred.

18
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.740
Winning the most votes is different from winning a

19
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:53.060
majority.

20
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.240
That is one concern motivating the change.

21
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.240
Election staff would explain a different replacement process.

22
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.200
If a legislator is recalled, a special election would

23
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.560
generally fill the seat.

24
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.180
Vacancies in most other state offices would be filled

25
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.040
by gubernatorial appointment.

26
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.900
So a separate replacement vote is not guaranteed for

27
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:15.600
every office.

28
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.460
For the governor, the lieutenant governor would take over.

29
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.520
If the recall happens early enough, before the specified

30
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.680
nomination deadline in the first two years, voters elect

31
00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.180
a new governor at a later statewide election.

32
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.540
Otherwise, the lieutenant governor completes the term.

33
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.960
Costs could rise if an additional special election is

34
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.740
needed, or fall when replacement-candidate materials disappear.

35
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.360
The analyst says the net effect is unknown, with

36
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.760
possible costs or savings of millions per recall.

37
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.880
A voter might want removal considered on its own,

38
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.960
rather than as a shortcut for a candidate with

39
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:53.860
limited support.

40
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.740
Familiar vacancy procedures could make succession more predictable.

41
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.920
That does not mean every replacement would be elected

42
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.980
in a new majority vote.

43
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.320
You might want to choose the replacement yourself, immediately.

44
00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.580
Another voter could object to giving appointments or automatic

45
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.780
succession a larger role.

46
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.080
They may prefer seeing the alternatives on the same

47
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.080
ballot, and worry that a later election leaves them

48
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:20.360
waiting.

49
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.700
These are choices about accountability, not just administrative convenience.

50
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.820
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 35

51
00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.710
percent said yes, 59 percent no, and 5 percent

52
00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:32.980
were unsure.

53
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:38.160
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

54
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.160
Yes changes the replacement process; no keeps the replacement

55
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.960
question on recall ballots.

56
00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.720
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

57
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.420
and official voter guide.

58
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.440
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
